<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muslim ibn Quraysh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1085">1085</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muslim ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-dawla">Sharaf al-Dawla</a>, was the Uqaylid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> from 1061 to 1085 and one of the leading Arab princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under Seljuk overlordship. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1080 and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> and other towns of the Jazira. He was killed in 1085 fighting Sulayman ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutalmish">Qutalmish</a>, the Seljuk ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muslim-ibn-quraysh.jpg" alt="Muslim ibn Quraysh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mustafa Wahhudi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Al-Durri_Shrine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muslim, whose full name was Abu'l-Makarim Muslim ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, was the son of Quraysh bin Badran, called Alam al-Din, the Arab emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. Quraysh acknowledged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> but later quarrelled with them, was for a time driven from Mosul, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> soldier Basasiri when he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> at the end of 1058. On his father's death in 1061 Muslim succeeded him. A Shiite, he at first recognised the Fatimid caliph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, but around 1066 he allied with the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> and married Safiyya, an aunt of the later Sultan Malik-Shah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1077 Muslim joined, as Malik-Shah's vassal, the campaign of the sultan's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush I</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, held by the Mirdasid Sabiq ibn Mahmud. He disliked the attack, persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kilab">Kilab</a> tribesmen to leave Tutush's army, let his troops sell food to the besieged city, and withdrew. On his way home he warned Sabiq of 1,000 Seljuk reinforcements at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a>, which an Arab coalition under Abu Za'ida Muhammad ibn Za'ida then routed at Wadi Butnan, and Tutush gave up the siege.</p><p>In 1080 Sabiq ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> to him, its people hoping he could protect them from Seljuk raids, and in the next year he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> from the Numayrids. He then fell out with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and was defeated by Malik-Shah's forces, but pardoned. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in December 1084, Muslim demanded the tribute that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a> had paid him, and Suleiman refused it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In June 1085 Muslim was killed fighting Suleiman's forces after his Turkoman mercenaries went over to the enemy and his Arab troops fled. His brother Ibrahim ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, released from prison, succeeded him, followed later by Muslim's son Ali ibn Muslim. Muslim was regarded as the last strong Arab king of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and the last Arab ruler whose lands included parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>; after his death Turkoman nomads came to dominate the region. The Imam Dur Mausoleum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> is said to be his tomb, though its inscription names Imam Muhammad al-Durri.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muslim ibn Quraysh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Weakened by internal feuds and Turkmen pressure, the dynasty lost Aleppo in 1080 to the Uqaylid <b>Muslim ibn Quraysh</b>, and the city soon passed to the Seljuk Sultanate of Syria.</blockquote><a href="/state/mirdasid-emirate">Mirdasid Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1081 the Uqaylid emir <b>Muslim ibn Quraysh</b> took Harran, ending Numayrid rule; the tribe itself survived in the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/numayrids">Numayrids</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Muslim ibn Quraysh</b>, called Sharaf al-Dawla, extended Uqaylid rule to Aleppo in 1080, making the dynasty a major power in Syria.</blockquote><a href="/state/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
